  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid and upper level low over the Eastern Great Lakes to track east\ninto New England overnight. Low clouds over the area will erode from\nwest to east late in the day thru this evening. In the wake of the\nlow a northwesterly flow aloft will develop with surface high\npressure over the Mid MS Valley building into the TN Valley\novernight. Clearing skies and light winds will result in chilly\novernight low temperatures. Expect lows to range from the upper 20s\nto the lower 30s. Have expanded the freeze warning for tonight into \nPickaway and Franklin counties where NBM probabilities show 50 to 70 \npercent chance for temperatures below 32 degrees. Even if \ntemperatures do not reach 32 in some urban locations - expect frost \nto develop. \n\n\nPrevious discussion... \nAn upper level low will track east through the Great Lakes \ninto New England today. This will place the Ohio Valley in a \nnorthwest flow pattern. Surface high pressure builds over MO today \nand tonight. Considerable cloud cover is expected in the cold \nadvection, along with a decent nw wind 10-15 mph. This is \nparticularly true in central OH closer to the cold pool.\n\nDecreasing winds combined with clear skies will lead to cool \novernight temperatures. Expect lows to be in the upper 20s to low \n30s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nHigh pressure will build to the south of the Ohio Valley and extend a\nridge into the western Appalachians, bringing a southwest flow to the\nregion. As a result, a dry forecast is expected through mid week \nwith highs moderating to a few degrees on either side of 60 Monday \nand Wednesday, peaking in the mid-upper 60s Tuesday.\n\nThe high will shift ewd and a cold front will cross the region later\nWednesday, ushering in high temperatures in the lower 50s for\nThursday, upper 40s Friday, and near 50 on Saturday.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, there is a lifting mechanism with a\nstrong low pressure lobe rotating around a strong swd diving low in\nthe Midwest. This initial low and subsequent broad low pressure\ncenter will bring a chance of rain to the region, primarily Thursday\nbut possibly starting Wed night in the srn and sern CWA.",
